-- Q11706: Contingencies [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 450-20-25, ''reasonably possible'' means the chance of the future event occurring is more than remote but less than likely. This is the middle tier of the three-level probability framework. Losses classified as reasonably possible require note disclosure but not accrual.
Wrong (B): ''Likely to occur'' describes the ''probable'' threshold, which triggers accrual when the amount is reasonably estimable. This is a higher likelihood than reasonably possible.
Wrong (C): A slight chance of occurrence describes ''remote,'' the lowest tier, which generally requires no disclosure or accrual.
Wrong (D): Virtually certain to occur overstates the threshold. Reasonably possible is below probable and far below virtual certainty.$EXPL$
