-- Q11709: Contingencies [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 450-20-25, a loss contingency is accrued when two conditions are both met: (1) it is probable that a loss has been incurred, and (2) the amount can be reasonably estimated. Both criteria must be satisfied simultaneously.
Wrong (A): The threshold is ''probable,'' not ''reasonably possible.'' Materiality is not a stated criterion for recognition under ASC 450.
Wrong (C): Confirmation by legal counsel and management approval are not the recognition criteria. ASC 450 focuses on probability and estimability, not internal approval processes.
Wrong (D): Relating to a past event and being due within one year are not the ASC 450 criteria. The standard requires probability and reasonable estimability regardless of timing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11709;

-- Q11710: Contingencies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 450-20, a loss contingency is accrued only when the loss remains probable and estimable. When the assessment changes from probable to reasonably possible, the accrual conditions are no longer met and the $1,800,000 liability is reversed as a change in estimate. The contingency must then be disclosed in the notes.
Wrong (A): Contingency accruals are reassessed each reporting period and reversed when the underlying conditions no longer support recognition. They are not permanent once recorded.
Wrong (B): Reclassifying to a ''disclosed commitment'' is not a recognized category. The accrual is reversed, and the contingency is disclosed as reasonably possible in the notes.
Wrong (D): Reducing to 50% has no basis in ASC 450. The standard does not use partial probability adjustments—the loss is either accrued in full or not accrued at all.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11710;

